      "title": "RBI absorbs ₹2.23 trillion via variable rate reverse repo auction",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/rbi-absorbs-2-23-trillion-via-variable-rate-reverse-repo-auction-126091800553_1.html",
      "description": "The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) on Friday absorbed Rs 2.23 lakh crore from the banking system amid surplus liquidity by conducting a variable rate reverse repo (VRRR) auction.\nThe central bank received bids worth Rs 2,22,629 crore, which it accepted fully, for a notified amount of Rs 2.25 lakh crore. The amount was accepted at a weighted average rate of 5.24 per cent, the RBI said in a release.\nThe RBI has been conducting VRRR auctions since last month in order to absorb excess liquidity from the banking system and align the overnight money market rates to the repo rate.\nAs of September 17, liquidity in the banking system is estimated to be in surplus of around Rs 6.94 lakh crore, as per RBI data.\nAs part of the liquidity adjustment strategy, the central bank on Thursday sold government securities worth Rs 50,000 crore through Open Market Operations (OMO).\nThe remaining two tranches of G-sec sales of Rs 25,000 crore each are scheduled for September 21 and September 28.\nThe bankin",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-18T09:53:07"
    },
    {
      "title": "India's net direct tax collection rises 13% to ₹12.12 trillion till Sept 17",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-s-net-direct-tax-collection-rises-13-to-12-12-trillion-till-sept-17-126091800557_1.html",
      "description": "The government's net direct tax collection rose 13 per cent to over Rs 12.12 lakh crore till September 17 of the current fiscal on higher advance tax mop-up, according to the latest data released on Friday.\nCorporate tax mop-up grew 19.48 per cent to about Rs 5.56 lakh crore, while non-corporate tax (individuals and HUFs) mop-up increased 6 per cent to over Rs 6.16 lakh crore. Securities Transactions Tax (STT) collection grew 53 per cent to Rs 40,214 crore between April 1 and September 17.\nRefund issuance jumped 29.19 per cent to over Rs 2.20 lakh crore till September 17, the data released by the Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) showed.\nGross direct tax collection clocked a 15 per cent growth totalling over Rs 14.32 lakh crore.\nAdvance Tax mop-up till September 17 increased 16.18 per cent to about Rs 5.22 lakh crore. This included an 18 per cent growth in advance corporate tax payments at over Rs 4.16 lakh crore, and a 9.24 per cent rise in non-corporate advance tax payments of",
